{"id":21050,"title":"AI Isn’t the New Threat. It’s the New Clock.","subtype":"webinar","standfirst":"Webinar","synopsisText":"In June, two compromised Azure service principals took 15 hours to map a tenant, then about seven minutes to delete most of its storage. Microsoft linked the attack to agentic tooling. The secret wasn’t novel, though. It sat in the edit history of a public GitHub issue, attached to a role that already had permission to destroy.\n\n\n\n\nMost of this year’s AI incidents follow the same pattern: agents leaking screenshots to public repos, unverified plugin pins, chatbots lending users their own permissions, and databases nobody knew were open. AI isn’t creating new weaknesses. It’s finding the old ones faster than we fix them. And the “AI is a new threat” framing gives security, product, and platform teams a pass on the basics.\n\n\n\n\nIn this session, Said Syed, Field CISO at Snyk, walks through the evidence and makes a contrarian case: the fundamentals haven’t changed. What has changed is how long you have to get them right. He closes with five moves for this quarter, all built to run at machine speed.\n\n\n\n\nYou’ll learn how to:\n\n\n\n\n 1. Kill exposed secrets first: a secret that was ever public is already compromised, so rotate it.\n 2. Apply least privilege to every identity: your bot should borrow the user’s access, never lend out its own.\n 3. Give every agent a named owner: if you can’t say who owns it, you can’t answer for what it does at 2am.\n 4. Validate independently, continuously: the model that wrote the code doesn’t get to grade it.\n 5. Close the loop at machine speed: a confirmed exploit should be one approval from fixed, not a ticket in a queue.\n\n\n\n\nWe don’t need to know where AI ends up. We need to accept that the clock changed and do the work we already know how to do.","sponsor":{"name":"Snyk","image":"https://regmedia.co.uk/2026/02/23/snyk_250x50_new.png","url":"https://snyk.io"},"iframeHtml":"","date":"2026-11-09T16:00:00.000Z","duration":60}